-a-manifold-is-the-zero-set-of-a-smooth-nonnegative-function","location":"[L1]","defect":"unlicensed-inference","evidence":"[L1] says that applying lem-a-countable-coordinate-ball-cover-has-a-countable-locally-finite-shrinking to the open manifold M\\setminus A supplies the stated W_k\\Subset V_k cover. The cited lemma's Statement instead begins: “Let (U_n)_{n\\ge 1} be a countable cover ... by coordinate balls with compact closures.” The corollary never constructs that prerequisite cover of M\\setminus A (the earlier open-cover lemma could do so, but is neither invoked nor cited here).","severity":"nonfatal"},{"id":"fs-curve-velocities-in-two-charts-have-the-same-coordinate-tuple","location":"2.1","defect":"unlicensed-inference","evidence":"Step 1.1 explicitly gives Cartesian velocity “(1,0)” and says that in polar coordinates the same curve “has derivative (1,0) in the (r,\\theta) variables.” Thus this chosen curve has identical coordinate tuples. The cited change-of-coordinate theorem only relates tuples by a Jacobian; it does not make these two equal tuples unequal. Step 2.1 therefore does not establish the claimed chart dependence from the supplied example.","severity":"fatal"},{"id":"lem-a-countable-coordinate-ball-cover-has-a-countable-locally-finite-shrinking","location":"2.1 and 3.1","defect":"unlicensed-inference","evidence":"Step 2.1 chooses each V_x only subject to x\\in W_x\\subseteq\\overline{W_x}\\subseteq V_x\\subseteq\\overline{V_x}\\subseteq U_n, then step 3.1 concludes that the collected (V_k) are locally finite. The cited lem-regularity-via-closed-neighbourhoods licenses only an open V with x\\in V\\subseteq\\overline V\\subseteq U; it provides no control keeping V_x near the annulus A_m. This omission is substantive: on \\mathbb R, take U_{2m-1}=(-1,m+1) and U_{2m}=(-m-1,1). For every odd annulus one may make the permitted choice V_x=(-1/2,m+1/2) at x=m; its closure lies in U_{2m-1}, yet all these V_x contain 0. Hence the choices allowed by step 2.1 need not be locally finite, so step 3.1 is not licensed.","severity":"fatal"},{"id":"thm-every-smooth-manifold-admits-a-smooth-proper-exhaustion-function","location":"2.1","defect":"unlicensed-inference","evidence":"Step 2.1 claims that “a point of K_m\\setminus K_{m-2} can meet only the supports of \\rho_{m-1},\\rho_m, and \\rho_{m+1}.” The support constraint from step 1.1 permits more indices. For M=\\mathbb R and K_n=[-n,n], take m=6 and p=4.5\\in K_6\\setminus K_4. A permissible \\rho_4 may have support reaching p while remaining inside int(K_5)\\setminus K_2=(-5,5)\\setminus[-2,2]; it is the m-2 term, not one of the asserted three. Local finiteness is still recoverable with a corrected finite-index argument, but the displayed justification is false.","severity":"nonfatal"}],"coverage_note":"Opened all 99 scoped items/pages exactly once and checked the direct cited dependencies needed for the audited inferences."}